{"author":"reconciler","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"OSS RESEARCH, PART 2 — GIT LOCK INTERNALS AND TOOLS THAT DRIVE GIT CONCURRENTLY (2026-07-31)\n\nCompanion to the previous comment. Deeper primary-source read. VERSION ANCHOR: git.git master @ a97fcc37c (2026-07-30); stable v2.55.0 (2026-06-29); prior v2.54.0 (2026-04-19). Two lock-relevant things landed RECENTLY and are easy to miss: core.lockfilePid (in 2.54+) and core.configLockTimeout (merged to master 2026-07-30, NOT YET IN ANY RELEASE).\n\n== 1. GIT ITSELF ==\n\n1.1 THE LOCKFILE MODEL — and the key negative finding. lockfile.h L4-31: \"Mutual exclusion and atomic file updates... we create a lockfile <filename>.lock, write the new file contents into it, and then rename the lockfile to its final destination... with O_CREAT|O_EXCL... Automatic cruft removal. If the program exits after we lock a file but before the changes have been committed, we want to make sure that we remove the lockfile. This is done by remembering the lockfiles we have created in a linked list and setting up an atexit(3) handler and a signal handler that clean up the lockfiles.\" Also: \"lockfiles only block other writers. Readers do not block, but they are guaranteed to see either the old contents of the file or the new contents.\" *** ON \"WHY DOES GIT DELIBERATELY NOT DO STALE-LOCK DETECTION\": THERE IS NO SUCH COMMENT, AND THAT IS ITSELF THE FINDING. *** lockfile.h and tempfile.h contain ZERO occurrences of \"stale\". The lockfile API has no concept of lock ownership, age, or liveness. Staleness is not rejected by an argued design note — it is simply OUTSIDE THE MODEL. A lock is a file that exists; cleanup is atexit+signal handlers in the owning process; anything surviving SIGKILL, OOM, power loss or container teardown is THE HUMAN'S PROBLEM. No mailing-list thread found arguing it either way.\n\n1.2 THE LOCK_* FLAGS (lockfile.h L155-178). There is NO \"LOCK_ON_ERROR\". The three are:\n  LOCK_DIE_ON_ERROR (1) — \"If a lock is already taken for the file, die() with an error message.\"\n  LOCK_NO_DEREF (2) — do not resolve symlinks; \".lock\" appended to the path argument itself.\n  LOCK_REPORT_ON_ERROR (4) — return -1 AND give the usual error message.\n  Default (flags 0) is SILENT -1.\n\n1.3 THE TIMEOUT MACHINERY. lockfile.h L180-190: \"If the file is currently locked, retry with quadratic backoff for at least timeout_ms milliseconds. If timeout_ms is 0, try exactly once; if timeout_ms is -1, retry indefinitely.\" Backoff is quadratic WITH JITTER, capped per-sleep at 1s. From commit 044b6a9ef \"lockfile: allow file locking to be retried with a timeout\" (Michael Haggerty, 2015-05-14): \"On each failed attempt, use select() to wait for a backoff time that increases quadratically (capped at 1 second) and HAS A RANDOM COMPONENT TO PREVENT TWO PROCESSES FROM GETTING SYNCHRONIZED.\" CONFIGURED TIMEOUTS (Documentation/config/core.adoc L580-598):\n  core.filesRefLockTimeout   ms  default 100    0=no retry  -1=forever   shipped 2.15+\n  core.packedRefsTimeout     ms  default 1000   0=no retry  -1=forever   shipped 2.5+\n  core.configLockTimeout     ms  default 1000   0=no retry  -1=forever   MASTER ONLY, UNRELEASED\n  credentialStore.lockTimeoutMS ms default 1000                          shipped 2.30+\nWHY THE TWO REF NUMBERS DIFFER — the load-bearing rationale, and it also explains the index. From f4ab4f3ab \"lock_packed_refs(): allow retries when acquiring the packed-refs lock\" (2015):\n  \"This is not such a limitation for loose reference files. First, they don't take long to rewrite.\n   Second, MOST REFERENCE UPDATES HAVE A KNOWN 'OLD' VALUE, so if another process is updating a\n   reference at the same moment that we are trying to lock it, then probably the expected 'old' value\n   will no longer be valid, and the update will fail anyway. But these arguments do not hold for\n   packed-refs: * The packed-refs file can be large and take significant time to rewrite. * Many\n   references are stored in a single packed-refs file...\"\nFrom 4ff0f01cb \"refs: retry acquiring reference locks for 100ms\" (2017), which walked that back:\n  \"The philosophy of reference locking has been, 'if another process is changing a reference, then\n   whatever I'm trying to do to it will probably fail anyway because my old-SHA-1 value is probably\n   no longer current'. But this argument falls down if the other process has locked the reference to\n   do something that DOESN'T ACTUALLY CHANGE THE VALUE of the reference, such as pack-refs or reflog\n   expire.\"\nHaggerty's operational number (mailing list): before the patch, \"maybe 10-50 such errors every day across our whole infrastructure\"; after, \"that number went basically to zero.\" Jeff King noted GitHub was already running equivalent logic.\n\n1.4 index.lock: CONFIRMED — NO RETRY KNOB EXISTS, ANYWHERE. repository.c L471-478 repo_hold_locked_index() -> repo_hold_lock_file_for_update(), defined in lockfile.h L236-241 as ..._timeout(..., 0) = TRY EXACTLY ONCE. All ~40 call sites (builtin/add.c:454, builtin/commit.c:534, builtin/checkout.c:630, apply.c:4966, add-interactive.c:645, ...) inherit that. git grep over Documentation/, *.c, *.h for indexLockTimeout|index_lock_timeout|INDEX_LOCK_TIMEOUT returns NOTHING. There is no core.indexLockTimeout and never has been. NO patch or RFC proposing one was found; the 100ms ref-lock thread contains no index discussion at all. GIT'S CHOSEN ANSWER INSTEAD — commit 27344d6a6 \"git: add --no-optional-locks option\" (Jeff King, 2017), the closest thing to an official position. It enumerates three options and REJECTS the coordination one:\n  \"1. Have some kind of 'pseudo-lock' that allows other commands to tell status that they want the\n      lock. THIS IS LIKELY TO BE COMPLICATED AND ERROR-PRONE TO IMPLEMENT (and maybe even impossible\n      with just dotlocks to work from, as it requires some inter-process communication).\n   2. Avoid background runs of commands like 'git status'... This is awkward...\n   3. Ask 'status' not to lock or write the index. This is easy to implement.\"\nAnd why index contention differs from ref contention: \"taking the index lock may conflict with other operations in the repository. Especially ones that the user is doing themselves, which _aren't_ opportunistic. In other words, 'git status' knows how to back off when somebody else is holding the lock, but other commands don't know that status would be happy to drop the lock if somebody else wanted it.\" SHIPPED MECHANISM: `git --no-optional-locks <cmd>` / GIT_OPTIONAL_LOCKS=0 (Documentation/git.adoc):\n  \"If this Boolean environment variable is set to false, Git will complete any requested operation\n   without performing any optional sub-operations that require taking a lock. For example, this will\n   prevent 'git status' from refreshing the index as a side effect. THIS IS USEFUL FOR PROCESSES\n   RUNNING IN THE BACKGROUND WHICH DO NOT WANT TO CAUSE LOCK CONTENTION with other operations on the\n   repository. Defaults to 1.\"\nIt is an ENV VAR deliberately, so it propagates to subprocesses (Peff). *** READING: git's answer to \"many agents driving one repo\" is NOT \"wait for the index lock\" — it is \"DON'T TAKE IT.\" ***\n\n1.5 WHAT GIT TELLS THE USER, AND THE 2026 PID-FILE CHANGE. Through v2.53 (unchanged since aed7480ca, 2016; wording origin e43a6fd3e, 2009 — \"Just saying that index.lock exists doesn't tell the user _what_ to do\"): \"Unable to create '%s.lock': %s.\\n\\nAnother git process seems to be running in this repository, e.g. an editor opened by 'git commit'. Please make sure all processes are terminated then try again. If it still fails, a git process may have crashed in this repository earlier: remove the file manually to continue.\" In v2.54+ replaced by dbdcab6b8 \"lockfile: add PID file for debugging stale locks\" (Paulo Casaretto, 2026-01-22). lockfile.c L254-299 picks one of three:\n  pid_status == 1  -> \"Lock may be held by process %ju; if no git process is running, the lock file\n                       may be stale (PIDs can be reused)\"\n  pid_status == -1 -> \"Lock was held by process %ju, which is no longer running; the lock file\n                       appears to be stale\"\n  else             -> \"Another git process seems to be running in this repository, or the lock file\n                       may be stale\"\nConfig core.lockfilePid (core.adoc L351-360), DEFAULT FALSE: \"Git will create a PID file alongside lock files... named by inserting ~pid before the .lock suffix... contains a single line in the format `pid <value>`.\" The `~` is deliberate (lockfile.h L132-137): \"The tilde is forbidden in refnames and allowed in Windows filenames, avoiding namespace collisions.\" THREE THINGS TO TAKE FROM THIS:\n 1. Even in 2026, with dead-PID detection in hand, GIT STILL DOES NOT BREAK THE LOCK. It prints\n    \"appears to be stale\" and stops. The commit is titled \"for DEBUGGING stale locks.\"\n 2. Git annotates its own PID-liveness check with \"(PIDs can be reused)\" IN USER-FACING TEXT. It does\n    not trust pid liveness as proof.\n 3. The PID file records NO HOSTNAME — unlike gc.pid. On a shared/network filesystem the kill(pid,0)\n    check is therefore meaningless-to-actively-wrong. Off by default, so latent, but a real gap.\n\n1.6 gc.pid — GIT'S OWN HOSTNAME+PID STALENESS SCHEME (the one place it does break a lock). builtin/gc.c L718-781, predicate decoded: gc backs off only if ALL hold — pidfile younger than 12h; parses as \"<pid> <hostname>\"; AND (host differs — in which case we cannot check, so ASSUME ALIVE — OR kill(pid,0) says the local pid is alive, OR errno==EPERM).\n  /* 12 hour limit is very generous as gc should never take that long... running gc --auto one day\n     late is not a big problem. --force can be used in manual gc after the user verifies that no gc\n     is running. */\n  time(NULL) - st.st_mtime <= 12 * 3600 &&\n  fscanf(fp, scan_fmt, &pid, locking_host) == 2 &&\n  /* be gentle to concurrent \"gc\" on remote hosts */\n  (strcmp(locking_host, my_host) || !kill(pid, 0) || errno == EPERM);\nFailure message (gc.c:999): \"gc is already running on machine '%s' pid %ju (use --force if not)\". Origin 64a99eb47 (2013): \"In the worst case, gc may be delayed 12 hours if a daemon reuses the pid stored in gc.pid.\" The design CONSCIOUSLY ACCEPTS A 12-HOUR FALSE-NEGATIVE WINDOW as the price of not racing. THREE STRUCTURAL SAFEGUARDS WORTH NAMING: (a) a WALL-CLOCK CEILING (12h) as backstop for any pid/hostname confusion; (b) an explicit HOSTNAME GUARD THAT FAILS CLOSED — a different hostname means \"assume running\", never \"assume stale\"; (c) errno==EPERM treated as alive (pid exists under another uid). CONTAINER CAVEAT, HONESTLY SCOPED: the mechanism has an obvious container failure mode (hostname is the ephemeral container/pod id, so a repo on a mounted volume gc'd from a NEW container after a hard kill sees strcmp != 0 -> fails closed -> gc refuses FOR UP TO 12 HOURS). The inverse also holds if hostnames are reused. NO git-project bug report, mailing-list thread, or upstream acknowledgement of either case was found. Treat the mechanism analysis as sound (read from source) but the CONTAINER INCIDENCE as UNDOCUMENTED, not a cited known issue. RELATED: `git maintenance` does NOT use this scheme — gc.c L1784-1800 takes a plain $GIT_OBJECT_DIRECTORY/maintenance.lock with LOCK_NO_DEREF: no pid, no hostname, no timeout, no staleness check. Same for schedule.lock at L3368. THE PID+HOSTNAME LOGIC IS `git gc` ONLY. gc.log: on --auto in daemonized mode stderr is captured into .git/gc.log.lock and committed to .git/gc.log iff non-empty (process_log_file(), gc.c L78-104). gc.logExpiry (config/gc.adoc L70-75): \"If the file gc.log exists, then 'git gc --auto' will print its content and exit with status zero instead of running unless that file is more than 'gc.logExpiry' old. Default is 1.day.\"\n\n1.7 DOES GIT EVER AUTO-REMOVE STALE LOCK FILES? Only its own, only in-process. atexit(3) + signal handlers (tempfile.c) roll back locks the CURRENT process holds, including on die(). Nothing scans for or reaps foreign locks. SIGKILL, OOM, power loss or container SIGKILL leaves the .lock in place PERMANENTLY. The only exceptions in the whole tree are gc's guarded gc.pid takeover and the gc.log unlink.\n\n== 2. TOOLS THAT DRIVE GIT CONCURRENTLY ==\n\n2.1 git-lfs — NO index.lock HANDLING AT ALL (a negative example). @b9964490 (2026-07-28). grep -rn \"index\\.lock\" over the tree: ZERO matches. grep -rni \"another git process\": zero. It never opens .git/index; it shells out to `git update-index -q --refresh --stdin` (git/git.go:645 UpdateIndexFromStdin), streamed through gitIndexer (commands/pull.go L185-234). Its ONLY concurrency guard is an in-process sync.Mutex serialising its own writes to that one subprocess's stdin. On failure it logs and continues (pull.go:153) — that string is what users see when the child dies on index.lock (#2937, #937, #2817). It has historically also CREATED the problem — #558 \"Failures during a checkout can leave the git index locked\": killing update-index mid-stream orphans the lock. A PR search for index.lock in git-lfs returns 0 results; NO ONE HAS EVER ADDED HANDLING. git-lfs does NOT use GIT_INDEX_FILE — the only occurrence is canonicalizeEnvironment() (commands/commands.go:391), which merely normalises an INHERITED value. So `git lfs pull`/`checkout` contend for the real index. The locking/ package is unrelated (server-side LFS file locks over HTTP/SSH). No OS advisory locks anywhere: grep -rniE \"flock|LOCK_EX\" finds nothing. The one retry loop is Windows sharing-violation handling (robustRemoveRetryDelaysMs = [0,1,10,20,40]).\n\n2.2 git-annex — THE STRONGEST DESIGN IN THE ECOSYSTEM. (DataLad mirror, upstream/master @ e3a0558c.) FIRST, THE STRUCTURAL MOVE: git-annex NEVER CONTENDS FOR .git/index.lock. It commits to the git-annex branch through a private index via GIT_INDEX_FILE — Git/Index.hs:17 (indexEnv = \"GIT_INDEX_FILE\"), Annex/GitOverlay.hs L26-63 (withIndexFile -> .git/annex/index), used by Annex/Branch.hs:688. SECOND, WHEN IT DOES BREAK A LOCK IT DOES SO UNDER A PROVEN INVARIANT, NOT A TIMER — Annex/Branch.hs L668-681 prepareModifyIndex: \"Since all modifications of the index are performed inside this module, and only when the journal is locked, the fact that the journal has to be locked when this is called ensures that no other process is currently modifying the index. So any index.lock file must be stale, caused by git running when the system crashed, or the repository's disk was removed, etc.\" => THE GENERAL PATTERN: A HIGHER-LEVEL LOCK ESTABLISHES THE INVARIANT THAT MAKES BREAKING THE LOWER-LEVEL LOCK SAFE. TWO BACKENDS. Utility/LockFile.hs disclaims portability: \"Posix and Windows lock files are extremely different. This module does *not* attempt to be a portability shim.\" Utility/LockFile/Posix.hs uses real fcntl record locks with two details worth stealing: tryLock runs under uninterruptibleMask_ so async exceptions cannot orphan a held fd; and checkSaneLock compares the fd's deviceID/fileID against a fresh stat of the path — \"This check is useful if the lock file might get deleted by something else\" (the classic fcntl unlink footgun). WHY PID LOCKS EXIST (man page): \"Normally, git-annex uses fine-grained lock files to allow multiple processes to run concurrently... That works great, unless you are using git-annex on a filesystem that does not support POSIX fcntl locks. This is sometimes the case when using NFS or Lustre... And, some networked filesystems are so inconsistent that one node can't reliably tell when the other node is holding a pid lock. CAVEAT EMPTOR.\" Defaults (Types/GitConfig.hs L255-257): annex.pidlock = False; annex.pidlocktimeout = 300 seconds. `git annex init` auto-enables by PROBING — taking a real exclusive lock in a temp dir and flipping the config on failure (Annex/Init.hs L458-464), with a 10s watchdog warning because the probe itself hangs on sick NFS. THE SIDE LOCK — THE GENUINELY NOVEL BIT. Joey Hess (devblog day 336): \"the fun part about pid locks is that it can be pretty hard to tell if one is stale or not. Especially when using a networked filesystem, because then the pid in question can be running on a different computer. Even if you do figure out that a pid lock is stale, how do you then take over a stale pid lock, without racing with another process that also wants to take it over?... PUT A MORE MODERN LOCK FILE SOMEPLACE ELSE (eg, /dev/shm) AND USE THAT LOCK FILE TO LOCK THE PID LOCK FILE. Then you can tell if a local pid lock file is stale quickly locally, and take it over safely.\" Mechanism (Utility/LockFile/PidLock.hs):\n - Pid-lock CONTENT is PidLock { lockingPid, lockingHost } — hostname recorded exactly because the pid\n   may be on another machine.\n - Side lock is a real POSIX exclusive lock in /dev/shm (fallback /tmp), i.e. ON LOCAL STORAGE THE\n   NETWORK FS CANNOT LIE ABOUT; name = abs pid-lock path with / -> _, md5-prefixed if truncated.\n   Deliberately world-writable: \"Since /dev/shm and /tmp are sticky dirs, a user cannot delete another\n   user's lock file there, so could not delete a stale lock.\"\n - RELEASE ORDERING IS LOAD-BEARING: dropSideLock UNLINKS BEFORE UNLOCKING — \"Delete the file first,\n   to ensure that any process that is trying to take the side lock will only succeed once the file is\n   deleted, and so will be able to immediately see that it's taken a stale lock\" — and dropLock drops\n   the side lock BEFORE the pid lock, \"at which point the pid lock will be considered stale.\"\n - TAKEOVER REQUIRES BOTH CONDITIONS: \"Since we have the sidelock, and are on the same host that the\n   pidlock was taken on, we know that the pidlock is stale, and can take it over.\" DIFFERENT HOSTNAME\n   => REFUSE, fall through to timeout. Residual limit stated plainly in the haddock: \"if the lock file\n   is on a networked file system, and was created on a different host than the current host\n   (determined by hostname), this can't be done and stale locks may persist.\"\n - Acquisition is link()-based per open(2)'s advice, verifying linkCount == 2 plus full stat equality,\n   with O_EXCL fallback where hardlinks are unsupported — plus an explicit LUSTRE INSANITY CHECK\n   (\"link has been observed to incorrectly *succeed*, despite the dest already existing\"), detected by\n   scanning the directory for two entries with the same name (devblog day 337).\n - CRITICALLY: linkToLock Nothing _ _ = return Nothing — WITHOUT THE SIDE LOCK IT WILL NOT EVEN\n   ATTEMPT ACQUISITION. The side lock is a PRECONDITION, not just a diagnostic.\nRETRY POLICY: FLAT 1-SECOND POLL, NO EXPONENTIAL BACKOFF, HARD ABORT. waitLock: \"Uses a 1 second wait-loop, retrying until a timeout. After the first second waiting, runs the callback to display a message, so the user knows why it's stalled\" -> \"waiting for pid lock file ... which is held by another process (or may be stale)\", then at exhaustion \"Gave up waiting for pid lock file ...\". TWO-TIER LOCKING (Utility/LockPool/PidLock.hs L34-43): \"There are two levels of locks. A STM lock is used to handle fine-grained locking among threads... The pid lock handles locking between processes.\" SELF-DEADLOCK ACROSS SUBPROCESSES is real and handled out-of-band: one pid lock per repo means a process holding it that spawns git, which invokes `git-annex filter-process`, deadlocks against itself. Fix is an env var PIDLOCK_<mangled-abs-path>=1 signalling \"your parent holds it\" (PidLock.hs L322-336), applied AD HOC — commit 0485dd316 explains why not globally: \"it would need to take the pid lock when running any and all processes, and that would be a problem when git-annex runs two processes concurrently.\"\n\n2.3 libgit2, dulwich, JGit/Gerrit, gitoxide. LIBGIT2 (@ddf3b5c8): NO retry, NO timeout, NO config support. src/util/filebuf.c L43-51 is one existence check + one O_CREAT|O_EXCL (futils.c L69-88), EEXIST -> GIT_ELOCKED. No loop, no sleep. grep for filesRefLockTimeout|packedRefsTimeout -> ZERO HITS; libgit2 does NOT honour git's timeout configs. Index message (index.c L3858-3866): \"the index is locked; this might be due to a concurrent or crashed process\". GIT_ELOCKED = -14. The de-facto contract is in libgit2's OWN TESTS — tests/libgit2/threads/refdb.c L45-93 wraps every call in `do { ... } while (error == GIT_ELOCKED);` — AN UNBOUNDED BUSY-SPIN WITH NO SLEEP, a model to note, not to copy. Maintainer position on stale locks (libgit2#809): cleanup deferred to the APPLICATION, with user approval. Live NFS contention bug: libgit2#6858 (open since Aug 2024, no maintainer response). Windows-only retry (do_with_retries, posix_w32.c L160-197) is for AV/sharing violations, not .lock contention. DULWICH (@d1c5d905): same, simpler. dulwich/file.py L210-234 — one os.open(O_RDWR|O_CREAT|O_EXCL), FileExistsError -> FileLocked. No retry, no sleep, and GitFile() HAS NO TIMEOUT PARAMETER TO PASS. Zero hits for the git timeout config names. FileLocked is raised and NEVER CAUGHT ANYWHERE in dulwich. The close() docstring names the hazard: \"If this method fails, it will attempt to delete the lockfile. However, it is not guaranteed to do so... which will prevent future writes to this file until the lockfile is removed manually.\" JGIT (@3f3beede) — PARTIAL. LockFile.createLockFileWithRetry() is MISLEADINGLY NAMED: one retry, only for NoSuchFileException (racing mkdirs), NOT for contention. The only contention backoff is packed-refs-only and HARDCODED — RefDirectory.java L123-125: RETRY_SLEEP_MS = (0,100,200,400,800,1600) (~3.1s, six attempts, then LockFailedException); the setter is package-private, NOT OPERATOR-CONFIGURABLE. Loose ref locks get nothing — RefUpdate returns Result.LOCK_FAILURE immediately. Zero hits for filesRefLockTimeout/packedRefsTimeout. JGit DOES add an in-process fair queue, and the comment is the best statement of the two-tier idea found (RefDirectory.java L157-171): \"This lock exists so multiple threads in the same process can wait in a fair queue without trying, failing, and retrying to acquire the on-disk lock.\" GERRIT — RETRIES THE OPERATION, NOT THE LOCK. THE MOST TRANSFERABLE PATTERN. ExceptionHookImpl.java L34-58: shouldRetry(...) { return isLockFailure(throwable); }. Consumed by RetryHelper with WaitStrategies.join(exponentialWait(maxWait=5s), randomWait(50ms)) under a 20s total budget, documented as retry.maxWait / retry.timeout in config-gerrit.txt: \"Maximum time to wait between attempts to retry an operation when one attempt fails (e.g. on NoteDb updates due to contention, aka lock failure, on the underlying ref storage). Operations are retried with exponential backoff, plus some random jitter... Defaults to 5 seconds.\" Gerrit preserves the causal chain so the retry can fire — MergeOp.java:1076: \"Lock failures are a special case: RetryHelper depends on this specific causal chain in order to trigger a retry.\" RETRYING THE WHOLE LOGICAL OP IS WHAT MAKES IT SAFE: it re-reads base state. GITOXIDE (@b95db7ca) is THE ONLY NON-C IMPLEMENTATION AT PARITY. gix-lock/src/acquire.rs L13-20:\n  pub enum Fail { #[default] Immediately, AfterDurationWithBackoff(Duration) }\n  /// Retry after failure with quadratically longer sleep times to block the current thread.\nquadratic-with-jitter backoff, error PermanentlyLocked { resource_path, mode, attempts } hinting \"The lockfile ... might need manual deletion\"; and it HONOURS BOTH GIT KEYS WITH GIT'S DEFAULTS (gix/src/config/cache/access.rs L220-238). SCOREBOARD: gitoxide = PARITY; JGit = PARTIAL + HARDCODED; libgit2 & dulwich = NOTHING.\n\n2.4 AVOIDANCE PATTERNS — DON'T TAKE THE LOCK. GIT_INDEX_FILE PER OPERATION. git(1): \"This environment variable specifies an alternate index file.\" Git's own canonical user is `git stash` — builtin/stash.c L502-521 states the reason verbatim:\n  /* We need to run restore files from a given index, but without affecting the current index, so we\n     use GIT_INDEX_FILE with run_command to fork processes that will not interfere. */\nplus in-process equivalents write_index_as_tree(..., stash_index_path.buf, ...), create_index_from_tree(), set_alternate_index_output(). `git filter-branch` does it globally (git-filter-branch.sh:286: GIT_INDEX_FILE=\"$(pwd)/../index\"; export GIT_INDEX_FILE) and its docs teach users the trick for --index-filter. git-annex uses it for its whole branch. libgit2's no-env equivalent is git_index_new() — \"Create an in-memory index object.\" *** WARNING: A PRIVATE INDEX REMOVES index.lock CONTENTION BUT NOT refs/**.lock CONTENTION — the final update-ref still hits the shared ref store. *** PURE PLUMBING, NO INDEX AT ALL: hash-object -w -> mktree (or scratch-index update-index --index-info + write-tree) -> commit-tree -> update-ref; only the last step locks, and only one ref. The cleanest in-tree proof is `git notes` — notes-utils.c L23-65 does write_notes_tree() -> commit_tree() -> refs_update_ref(), CREATING NO index.lock EVER. Application exemplars: git-appraise (Google) stores review data in git-notes under refs/notes/devtools, \"Each item stored is written as a single line of JSON\" — line-per-item PRECISELY SO CONCURRENT WRITES MERGE rather than conflict; GitHub's Git Data API is this model over HTTP (blobs->trees->commits->refs, base_tree for incremental builds, no checkout); josh rewrites whole histories via libgit2 treebuilders, with its single repo.index() call confined to the explicit \"snapshot my dirty worktree\" input mode. git worktree PARTITIONS THE INDEX, NOT THE REFS. git-worktree docs: each linked worktree gets its own \"files such as HEAD, index, etc.\", but \"In general, all pseudo refs are per-worktree and all refs starting with refs/ are shared... SINCE REFS ARE SHARED ACROSS ALL WORKTREES, except refs/bisect, refs/worktree and refs/rewritten.\" And \"By default, the repository config file is shared across all worktrees\" — so config.lock is shared t … [truncated by reconciler]","jira_comment_id":"985594"},"env_id":"reconciler","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1787092889639327191,"uuid":"a9b5cf17-2cfb-4630-81ea-f6f986aa3e57"}